\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Plaquette signs and the two torus holonomies}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{On the \vocabk{square torus}{fdip-006M}, let
#{G=\mathbb Z_2=\{1,-1\}} under multiplication, with
#{n=|V|=P} and #{|E|=2n}. The map sending a connection to its
plaquette signs has image exactly
##{\left\{(u_p)\in\{1,-1\}^{\mathcal P}:\prod_pu_p=1\right\}.}
Every such sign configuration has #{2^{n+1}} preimages in #{G^E}.
It consists of 4 gauge orbits, distinguished by two fundamental
cycle holonomies.}

\proof{
\p{Each edge occurs twice among the square boundaries, so
#{\prod_pU_p=1}. Conversely, choose a spanning tree in the connected
dual graph, whose vertices are squares and whose edges cross their
shared primal edges. Root this tree and set all primal edge signs to
#{1}. Process dual leaves towards the root. At each leaf, its unique
remaining dual-tree edge crosses a primal edge shared with its parent.
Flip that primal sign exactly when needed to give the leaf its
prescribed plaquette sign, then remove the leaf. This changes only the
leaf and its parent among the unprocessed squares and changes no
previously fixed square. At the last square, the global product
constraint forces the prescribed root sign. This constructs every
even-parity plaquette pattern.}

\p{The plaquette map is a homomorphism between finite abelian groups.
Multiplication by one preimage identifies its kernel with any nonempty
fiber. There are #{2^{P-1}} target patterns, hence each fiber has
##{\frac{2^{|E|}}{2^{P-1}}=2^{n+1}}
elements. For a connected graph, a gauge transformation fixes an
abelian connection exactly when #{g_{h(e)}=g_{s(e)}} on every edge,
that is, when all vertex values are the same. The stabilizer has two
elements, so every gauge orbit has #{2^{n-1}} elements. The fiber
therefore contains #{2^{n+1}/2^{n-1}=4} orbits.}

\p{Take a horizontal fundamental cycle in one row and a vertical one
in one column. Multiply by #{-1} every horizontal edge crossing the
periodic seam from #{x=N_1-1} to #{x=0}. Each plaquette contains zero
or two such edges, so its sign is unchanged, while the horizontal
cycle holonomy flips and the vertical one does not. The analogous
vertical seam operation flips just the other holonomy. The two
operations give 4 possibilities for these gauge-invariant signs.
They are distinct gauge orbits, and the count above shows that they
exhaust the 4 orbits in each plaquette fiber.}
}
